Germany","World War, 1939-1945 -- Art and the war","World War, 1939-1945 -- Destruction and pillage -- Europe","Art treasures in war -- Europe -- History -- 20th century","Cultural property -- Protection -- Europe -- History -- 20th century"],"publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Robert M. Edsel with Bret Witter.","oclc_numbers":["317118312"],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"key":"/books/OL23192583M","works":[{"key":"/works/OL8847112W"}],"number_of_pages":473,"latest_revision":23,"revision":23,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2009-05-15T04:47:45.155948"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2025-11-07T16:42:41.916807"}},{"publishers":["Center Street"],"subtitle":"Allied Heroes, Nazi Thieves and the Greatest Treasure Hunt in History","description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"From the Author's Note...\r\n\r\nWhat if I told you there was a group of men on the front lines who quite literally saved the world as we know it; a group that didn’t carry machine guns or drive tanks, who weren’t official statesmen; men who not only had the vision to understand the grave threat to the greatest cultural and artistic achievements of civilization, but then joined the front lines to do something about it?\r\n\r\nThese unknown heroes were known as the “Monuments Men,” a group of soldiers who served in the Western Allied military effort from 1943 until 1951. Their initial responsibility was to mitigate combat damage, primarily to structures—churches, museums, and other important monuments. As the war progressed and the German border was breached, their focus shifted to locating movable works of art and other cultural items stolen or otherwise missing. During their occupation of Europe, Hitler and the Nazis pulled off the “greatest theft in history,” seizing and transporting more than five million cultural objects to the Third Reich. The Western Allied effort, spearheaded by the Monuments Men, thus became the “greatest treasure hunt in history,” with all the unimaginable and bizarre stories that only war can produce."},"covers":[6409124],"physical_format":"Electronic resource","key":"/books/OL24260950M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL3033845A"}],"table_of_contents":[{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Cover","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Title Page","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Welcome","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Dedication","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Epigraph","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Author’s Note","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Main Characters","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"","title":"Map","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"I","title":"THE MISSION","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"1","title":"Out of Germany","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"2","title":"Hitler’s Dream","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"3","title":"The Call to Arms","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"4","title":"A Dull and Empty World","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"5","title":"Leptis Magna","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"6","title":"The First Campaign","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"7","title":"Monte Cassino","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"8","title":"Monuments, Fine Arts, and Archives","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"9","title":"The Task","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"II","title":"NORTHERN EUROPE","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":2,"label":"10","tit